#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166585 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/id #uuid "285e2f4a-e8d0-5f5e-a2d2-fe5ba8476358" :question/math-academy-id "e-8256" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\cos(xy), x=t,$ and $y=e^t,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x = x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}t} \n= \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=1,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =[-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot 1+[-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})]\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t})-t{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =-(1+t){e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t{e}^{t}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166586 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/id #uuid "7da1ada0-43e5-5807-b2c2-2a254a76cf27" :question/math-academy-id "e-8257" :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = 2\\sqrt{x+y}$ and $y=\\cos x,$ compute $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y = y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}f}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+y}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}-\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\sqrt{x+\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166587 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "1491963b-fbd8-57fa-bdde-f2d6705fbb83" :question/math-academy-id "e-8258" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y) = x^2 + y^2,$ where $x = s\\cos{t}$ and $y = t\\sin{s}.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y = y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡s.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(-s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡s)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡s) \\\\  & =-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2t{sin}^{2}⁡s \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-2{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2t{sin}^{2}⁡s-{s}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the identity $\\sin 2t = 2\\sin t\\cos t$ in the above simplification."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166588 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/id #uuid "e535fe44-a3cb-5c11-831c-118b90582c0d" :question/math-academy-id "e-8259" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z) = e^{xy + z},$ where $x = s + t,$ $y = s-t$ and $z = -s^2.$ Calculate $\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/b5/b593a1b60fe8c1aef2c5c229ff426db350e6296b9ecb767494283f0fa3342072.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t), y = y(s,t)$ and $z = z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial t} = \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial x}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial y}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial t} + \\dfrac{\\partial f}{\\partial z}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial t}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x, y$ and $z\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=x{e}^{xy+z},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}={e}^{xy+z}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(s-t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(s+t){e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}=(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & ={e}^{(s+t)(s-t)-{s}^{2}}={e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂t} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =(s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 1+(s+t){e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot (-1)+{e}^{-{t}^{2}}\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =(s-t-s-t){e}^{-{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =-2t{e}^{-{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437273 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "125ca586-3337-5120-876d-92e40e11dbf5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437260} {:answer/id #uuid "ba33cf18-d667-5a40-9d16-6cf17e9bc306" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437263} {:answer/id #uuid "dacbed10-7956-5d58-b766-2a05b8deb073" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡x{cos}^{2}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437266} {:answer/id #uuid "dacc660c-f7e4-5ae5-93dd-d34894e5734f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437267} {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3c8e0-fd65-53d1-b833-3d4e2653e49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437269}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f1c3c8e0-fd65-53d1-b833-3d4e2653e49b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437269} :answer-field/id #uuid "f729b4c9-529c-5fb2-a0e6-3a129de28dfc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437271}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ab9cce6f-4b79-504d-bebd-6e1b2e2dc75d" :question/math-academy-id "q-170671" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{sin}⁡x$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=(1-{y}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-2y\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =(1-{cos}^{2}⁡x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡x={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & ={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+(-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x) \\\\  & ={sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x+2{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323885 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9a8991-24fd-50f9-8941-a329e3133c45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+10{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321230} {:answer/id #uuid "84b790fb-dce2-5ea5-9fbd-fb66967b78c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-20s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322195} {:answer/id #uuid "bb1f027a-f67b-5d1f-b407-9ee3b85b3667" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322598} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bfc905-fde6-54a5-83ad-c5fa1e325d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} {:answer/id #uuid "f675ee7a-8c5d-5a9a-90cb-d0e4dd971fe3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{s}^{2}{t}^{4}-15s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8bfc905-fde6-54a5-83ad-c5fa1e325d7a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322709} :answer-field/id #uuid "af1de9ae-38d9-5d66-a275-9fc76c319cbf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323509}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd5b5db6-0961-5b1c-8f50-24c2f4348bc1" :question/math-academy-id "q-37132" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}y-5yz,$ where $x=st,$ $y={s}^{3}$ and $z=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}-5z,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=-5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(st)({s}^{3})=2{s}^{4}t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(st)^{2}-5(s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)={s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =-5{s}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =3{s}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2{s}^{4}t)\\cdot t+({s}^{2}{t}^{2}-5s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\cdot 3{s}^{2}+(-5{s}^{3})\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2{s}^{4}{t}^{2}+3{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-15{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-5{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =5{s}^{4}{t}^{2}-20{s}^{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186437272 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5011f4d0-0c40-5764-9ee2-dff49de39782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437261} {:answer/id #uuid "639601fb-cc44-52a3-bcb1-718c744fd6ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437262} {:answer/id #uuid "cbb75712-99aa-5d77-a9d1-0d236df66a51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437264} {:answer/id #uuid "cd89300c-6a2b-5a80-9770-886f817b90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437265} {:answer/id #uuid "f132c264-24c8-5558-941c-11fddb4def73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437268}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd89300c-6a2b-5a80-9770-886f817b90fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437265} :answer-field/id #uuid "c152aa95-4d94-5d9c-b5bb-30ac91e12518" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186437270}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "00d74228-1684-517b-8b3d-a3ad6a19bdf9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94745" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=x-y,$ where $x=-s\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ and $y=s\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/21/21cec766fe72965697304ef4288ffb2b090ddfca179210165dbcfd42ec490b27.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=1,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =1, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s} \\\\  & =(1)(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)+(-1)(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =-(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385824 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166749 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f26d941b-b4b4-591e-b7e0-ae4bbf81e94b" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "010a1725-78d9-5ef6-976d-66b572c1b87d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ts({t}^{2}-3ts+2{s}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385826} {:answer/id #uuid "3a12b2ee-d263-5ffd-96bf-c7a89479c594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s{t}^{2}(s-2t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385827} {:answer/id #uuid "cd01d3dd-a324-5561-83a5-466a5fba3616" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t({s}^{2}+s+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385828} {:answer/id #uuid "bc3eb310-caaa-5a1e-9eea-e12deb4203bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "ts(1+ts)(1-st)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385829} {:answer/id #uuid "39c971fc-9574-59c2-93dc-706cf5f69912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{s}^{2}(2s-3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385830}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39c971fc-9574-59c2-93dc-706cf5f69912" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t{s}^{2}(2s-3t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385830} :answer-field/id #uuid "722f5fc1-8625-5de3-b976-ddcdf5183ff0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385825}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cddd99f6-007e-52c0-bac7-f496a002a8cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-94748" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y)=y{x}^{2},$ where $x=ts$ and $y=s-t.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/11/118759b163a4410f66d5855d7b7b1fd9c2ee4be585e778a759997036bd95f765.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t)$ and $y=y(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2xy,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(ts)(s-t)=2t{s}^{2}-2s{t}^{2}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(ts)^{2}={t}^{2}{s}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂t} & =s, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂t} & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂t} \\\\  & =(2t{s}^{2}-2s{t}^{2})(s)+({t}^{2}{s}^{2})(-1) \\\\  & =2t{s}^{3}-2{s}^{2}{t}^{2}-{t}^{2}{s}^{2} \\\\  & =2t{s}^{3}-3{t}^{2}{s}^{2} \\\\  & =t{s}^{2}(2s-3t).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166750 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "71c243ca-5d46-58d2-bd16-28075c04d2db" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Function of Three Intermediate Variables and Two Independent Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7c63ba2d-fffd-5ea7-ac15-33befec87a20" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "s(t+s)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358827} {:answer/id #uuid "999f2f54-67a3-50e6-b7d4-c629f08c9bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "t(5-s)^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359100} {:answer/id #uuid "c7cc85c1-989f-5e4b-99f2-dde3a379fedd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4(st+s-t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359509} {:answer/id #uuid "dcc0bc08-7a90-588c-9d33-3108cf46a58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5s-t+s{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359690} {:answer/id #uuid "ec51930c-d788-57bf-9004-06129c3fa9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3s-5t-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359857}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcc0bc08-7a90-588c-9d33-3108cf46a58f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2(5s-t+s{t}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359690} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc844387-da57-56cb-b663-9a5de9135b9f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360446}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e1a4443a-d602-5476-a3eb-e3b0f2ea7373" :question/math-academy-id "q-94815" :question/problem "Let $f(x,y,z)={x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2},$ where $x=t+2s,$ $y=3t-s$ and $z=st.$ Calculate $\\frac{∂f}{∂s}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/be/be6afcdd0ad9ae0d7ef377c4918442a64e1186c90c25e27ec6c224d816a602fd.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y,z)$ with two independent variables $s$ and $t,$ where $x=x(s,t),y=y(s,t)$ and $z=z(s,t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂f}{∂s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{∂x}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{∂y}{∂s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂z}\\cdot \\frac{∂z}{∂s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x,y$ and $z:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=2y,\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂z}=2z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s$ and $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(t+2s)=2t+4s, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =2(3t-s)=6t-2s, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂z} & =2st.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x,$ $y,$ and $z$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂x}{∂s} & =2, \\\\ \\frac{∂y}{∂s} & =-1, \\\\ \\frac{∂z}{∂s} & =t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂s} & =(2t+4s)\\cdot 2+(6t-2s)\\cdot (-1)+(2st)\\cdot t \\\\  & =4t+8s-6t+2s+2s{t}^{2} \\\\  & =10s-2t+2s{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2(5s-t+s{t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360551 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0906d5df-95ee-50f2-a42b-09e69555e339" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t(1+2t){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357833} {:answer/id #uuid "140ea72d-9a0d-57b5-b67c-2049bb317343" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{t}^{2}(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186357925} {:answer/id #uuid "9cea513d-612f-5290-b731-384d792ca237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359124} {:answer/id #uuid "c0ba0846-4e92-5a2d-b302-7d756aa55faa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8{t}^{3}(2+t){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359440} {:answer/id #uuid "e95d1d3f-a370-59d0-97ce-9062ff9fd3d0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4t(2+t){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359826}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9cea513d-612f-5290-b731-384d792ca237" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359124} :answer-field/id #uuid "d9bfd21b-4c44-5c5c-ba56-5fadb4ae0273" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360377}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4d8c80e5-aa7d-56a9-83f4-b1c7ab4b5caf" :question/math-academy-id "q-95076" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)={x}^{2}{e}^{y},x=2t$ and $y={t}^{2},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f0/f01b253f560405a8c0f4be8f8797c652fb0ddf774f4db110b8822313b8ac50f3.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $t,$ where $x=x(t)$ and $y=y(t),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=2x{e}^{y},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={x}^{2}{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $t,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =2(2t){e}^{{t}^{2}}=4t{e}^{{t}^{2}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =(2t)^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}=4{t}^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $t,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2,\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=2t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =4t{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2+4{t}^{2}{e}^{{t}^{2}}\\cdot 2t \\\\  & =8t{e}^{{t}^{2}}+8{t}^{3}{e}^{{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =8t(1+{t}^{2}){e}^{{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186385838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166747 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5ed13b87-51ab-547d-a9ed-960eda7db24e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "247ee7d0-904c-5d01-8297-06a98015037c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{s}}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385840} {:answer/id #uuid "7cec0dc1-33bb-5f04-a034-05527df9b19d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+s){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385841} {:answer/id #uuid "323b91bd-668a-507d-8f83-8a04b4f2b115" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(2+\\sqrt{s}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385842} {:answer/id #uuid "ffd6bb8a-7411-5765-b787-90052e6b40f0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385843} {:answer/id #uuid "0fbb05f2-b967-534b-9feb-494c22d63500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0fbb05f2-b967-534b-9feb-494c22d63500" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385844} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f70704d-2718-514e-b670-1b5ed75416ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186385839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c583feca-7024-59b3-b46e-988493997541" :question/math-academy-id "q-95083" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=y{e}^{x},$ $x=\\sqrt{s}$ and $y=s,$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}$ for $s>0.$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e5b5001794ecdf2c9b9e9102f334a63d2c59bc6f7511c2eb7fa358fc94cb0c5.png)\n\nThe chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ with one independent variable $s,$ where $x=x(s)$ and $y=y(s),$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=y{e}^{x},\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}={e}^{x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $s,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =s{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}, \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & ={e}^{\\sqrt{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivatives of $x$ and $y$ with respect to $s,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}},\\,\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting all of our derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}s} & =\\frac{∂f}{∂x}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}s}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}s} \\\\  & =s{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{s}}+{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}{e}^{\\sqrt{s}}+{e}^{\\sqrt{s}} \\\\  & =(1+\\frac{\\sqrt{s}}{2}){e}^{\\sqrt{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186360570 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186166748 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1457941c-efa8-556f-9df7-3c5ae7b44ced" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Derivative of a Multivariable Function With One Independent Variable in the Special Case"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "23dbcf33-c576-54cf-8c07-43b62cf69983" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1+4{x}^{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358069} {:answer/id #uuid "558df00d-6a5e-5bb3-bb8a-e8288e5b1e90" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1-4{x}^{3}}{x})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358497} {:answer/id #uuid "67861fb7-92d5-5f5e-8f74-f8ff3c7f315c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1-3{x}^{3})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{2}+1}{{x}^{2}})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358655} {:answer/id #uuid "70539894-ff4f-5b7d-809c-ae8c476c88fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358729} {:answer/id #uuid "da024232-5563-5f22-a044-1622a067232a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2{x}^{2}+1}{x})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186359659}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70539894-ff4f-5b7d-809c-ae8c476c88fc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358729} :answer-field/id #uuid "f550e8b8-0dda-5ff3-ba5c-bfed41503899" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186360429}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5f396a56-19fc-58e7-855c-d3a310fbb9ec" :question/math-academy-id "q-95119" :question/problem "If $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2}+y)$ and $y=\\frac{1}{x},$ compute $\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule for a multivariable function $f(x,y)$ in the case where $y=y(x)$ can be expressed as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{∂f}{∂x}+\\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the partial derivatives of $f$ with respect to $x$ and $y:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x}=-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y),\\,\\frac{∂f}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWriting the above results in terms of $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}), \\\\ \\frac{∂f}{∂y} & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we take the derivative $y$ with respect to $x,$ and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, putting the derivatives into the multivariable chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x} & =-2x\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x})+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}))\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-2x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{x}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1-2{x}^{3}}{{x}^{2}})\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{{x}^{3}+1}{x}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
